Below is a step‑by‑step recipe for turning a single scanned JPG (or any georeferenced raster image) into a **MapLibre `image` source**, adding it as a raster layer, and exposing a simple opacity toggle.  
The pattern works for any static image when you know the four corner coordinates in **lon/lat (WGS‑84)**.

---

## 1. Why an `image` source?

| Source type | When it fits | Pros | Cons |
|-------------|--------------|------|------|
| **`image`** | One‑off raster (scanned map, historic map, aerial photo) where you have the four corner coordinates. | • No tile server needed.<br>• Simple JSON config.<br>• Works out‑of‑the‑box with MapLibre GL JS.<br>• Can be toggled, faded, blended with other layers. | • Not tiled → pixelates when you zoom far beyond the native resolution.<br>• No on‑the‑fly reprojection (must be in EPSG:3857 / WGS‑84). |
| **`raster` tiles** | Large images that need to stay crisp at many zoom levels, or you want to serve them via a tile server/PMTiles. | • Tiles stay sharp at any zoom you generate.<br>• Can be cached/CDN‑friendly. | • Requires a tile‑generation step (gdal2tiles, tippecanoe‑raster, maptiler, etc.) and a server/PMTiles host. |

Because you have a single JPG and you only need to overlay it at its original scale, the `image` source is the **right choice**. If later you discover the image looks blurry at higher zooms, you can always re‑tile it and switch to a `raster` source – the rest of the style stays the same.

---

## 2. Prepare the corner coordinates

MapLibre expects the corners in **counter‑clockwise order** starting with the **top‑left** (north‑west) corner:

```text
[
  [lonNW, latNW],   // top‑left
  [lonNE, latNE],   // top‑right
  [lonSE, latSE],   // bottom‑right
  [lonSW, latSW]    // bottom‑left
]
```

If you only have the four corners in a different order, just reorder them.  
*Tip:* you can verify the placement quickly with a small HTML page that draws a `Polygon` using those coordinates and checks it against a basemap.

---

## 3. Add the source & layer in JavaScript

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Historical map overlay</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    #opacity-control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.8);
      padding: 8px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<div id="opacity-control">
  <label for="opacity">Historical map opacity:</label>
  <input type="range" id="opacity" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0.7">
</div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map (use any basemap you like)
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY', // or any vector style URL
    center: [/* lon */, /* lat */],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 2️⃣ When the style is ready, add the image source + raster layer
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // ---- IMAGE SOURCE -------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('historical-map', {
      type: 'image',
      url: 'https://example.com/path/to/1920s-city-map.jpg', // <-- your JPG URL
      // Replace the four corners with your actual coordinates:
      coordinates: [
        [-122.523, 37.833], // top‑left (NW)
        [-122.355, 37.833], // top‑right (NE)
        [-122.355, 37.704], // bottom‑right (SE)
        [-122.523, 37.704]  // bottom‑left (SW)
      ]
    });

    // ---- RASTER LAYER -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historical-map-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historical-map',
      paint: {
        // Start semi‑transparent; the UI will modify this value.
        'raster-opacity': 0.7
      }
    });

    // ---- OPTIONAL: put the raster *under* the basemap labels -----
    // If the basemap style has a layer called "road-label" (common in OSM‑based styles)
    // you can insert the raster just before the first symbol layer so labels stay on top.
    const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
    if (firstSymbol) {
      map.moveLayer('historical-map-layer', firstSymbol.id);
    }
  });

  // 3️⃣ Opacity UI – sync the range input with the raster layer
  const opacitySlider = document.getElementById('opacity');
  opacitySlider.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
    const opacity = parseFloat(e.target.value);
    map.setPaintProperty('historical-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **`type: 'image'`** | Declares a single‑image source. MapLibre will request the JPG once and keep it in memory. |
| **`url`** | Publicly reachable URL (CORS must allow `GET`). If the image lives on the same origin as your page you’re fine; otherwise add `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your domain) on the server. |
| **`coordinates`** | The four corner points in lon/lat, counter‑clockwise. MapLibre internally projects them to Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). |
| **`type: 'raster'` layer** | Renders the image source as a raster tile. The only paint property you’ll need is `raster-opacity`. |
| **`moveLayer`** (optional) | Places the raster *under* any symbol layers (labels, icons) that the basemap provides, so the historic map doesn’t hide them. |
| **Opacity slider** | Calls `map.setPaintProperty()` on every input event – instantly updates the map without a reload. |

---

## 4. Things to watch out for

| Issue | How to avoid / fix |
|-------|--------------------|
| **CORS errors** | The JPG must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` that includes your page’s origin (or `*`). If you control the server, add that header. If you can’t, host the image on a CORS‑friendly CDN (e.g., GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare R2). |
| **Wrong corner order** | The image will appear rotated or flipped. Double‑check the order (NW, NE, SE, SW). A quick sanity check: set the opacity to `1` and compare the overlay to a known basemap – the corners should line up exactly. |
| **Pixelation at high zoom** | The image is a single raster; zooming beyond its native resolution will blur. If you need crisp detail at, say, zoom 15+, generate raster tiles (e.g., with `gdal2tiles.py` or `maptiler`) and switch the source type to `raster`. The layer definition stays the same; only the source definition changes. |
| **Large image size** | Browsers keep the whole image in memory. Keep the JPG under ~10 MB for smooth mobile performance. If the scan is huge, downscale it first (e.g., with ImageMagick: `convert input.tif -resize 4096x4096 output.jpg`). |
| **Projection mismatch** | MapLibre works in Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). The corner coordinates must be in WGS‑84 lon/lat. If you have them in a different CRS (e.g., NAD83 / State Plane), convert them first (proj4, GDAL `cs2cs`, or an online converter). |
| **Opacity toggle UI** | The example uses a simple `<input type="range">`. In a real app you might want a button that toggles between “on/off” (`opacity = 0` vs. previous value) or a UI library (React, Vue, etc.) that binds the slider to a state variable. The underlying call is always `map.setPaintProperty('historical‑map‑layer', 'raster‑opacity', value)`. |

---

## 5. When you *do* need tiles instead of a single image

If after testing you find the historic map looks too blurry at the zoom levels your users need, you can convert the JPG into a tiled raster set and keep the same layer name:

1. **Generate tiles** (choose a tile size, usually 256 px):
   ```bash
   # Using GDAL (works for GeoTIFF, PNG, JPG)
   gdal_translate -of GTiff -a_srs EPSG:4326 \
       -a_ullr lonNW latNW lonSE latSE input.jpg tmp.tif

   gdal2tiles.py -z 0-15 -w none tmp.tif ./tiles
   ```
   *or* use **MapTiler**, **TileServer‑GL**, or **PMTiles** for a serverless bundle.

2. **Host the tiles** (static file host, CDN, or PMTiles file served via `pmtiles` protocol).

3. **Swap the source definition**:
   ```js
   map.addSource('historical-map', {
     type: 'raster',
     tiles: ['https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
     tileSize: 256,
     // optional: minzoom / maxzoom if you only generated a subset
   });
   ```

   The layer (`type: 'raster'`) stays exactly the same, so you don’t need to touch any UI code.

---

## 6. Full checklist (quick copy‑paste)

- [ ] Image URL reachable **with CORS**.
- [ ] Corner coordinates in **lon/lat**, ordered **NW‑NE‑SE‑SW**.
- [ ] Add `image` source → `raster` layer.
- [ ] (Optional) Move layer **below** first symbol layer for label visibility.
- [ ] Add UI that calls `map.setPaintProperty(..., 'raster-opacity', value)`.
- [ ] Test on desktop **and** a mobile device (watch for lag > 2 s → consider tiling).
- [ ] If needed, generate raster tiles and replace the source with a `raster` tile source.

---

### TL;DR code snippet (copy‑paste)

```js
// After map style is loaded
map.addSource('historical-map', {
  type: 'image',
  url: 'https://example.com/1920s-city.jpg',
  coordinates: [
    [lonNW, latNW],
    [lonNE, latNE],
    [lonSE, latSE],
    [lonSW, latSW]
  ]
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'historical-map-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'historical-map',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.7 }
});

// UI hook
document.getElementById('opacity')
        .addEventListener('input', e =>
          map.setPaintProperty('historical-map-layer',
                               'raster-opacity',
                               parseFloat(e.target.value)));
```

That’s all you need to overlay a scanned historic map, let users fade it in/out, and keep the rest of your MapLibre map (vector basemap, labels, terrain, etc.) untouched. Happy mapping!

